# Repo-root .gitattributes
#
# Locked rules for files whose BYTE CONTENT must stay identical
# across checkouts on different platforms. Anything not listed here
# falls back to git's default `text=auto` behaviour.
#
# Why this exists: v0.15.1 shipped to beta with a CRLF-converted
# copy of `V0006__history_speaker_names.sql` baked into the binary
# via refinery's `embed_migrations!`. v0.15.0 had been built from
# the same file in its LF form. Refinery hashes the migration's
# SQL content and compares against the hash recorded in the user's
# DB; LF vs CRLF produces different hashes; v0.15.1 panicked at
# bootstrap with "applied migration V6__history_speaker_names is
# different than filesystem one V6__history_speaker_names" on
# every machine that had run v0.15.0 first.
#
# The git-stored bytes are LF on every commit (see
# `git ls-files --eol`). The bug was a Windows working-tree
# rewrite via `core.autocrlf=true` between two `tauri build`
# invocations. Pinning `eol=lf` here forces the working tree to
# match the index regardless of the local autocrlf setting.

# Refinery-bundled migrations. NEVER touch these without
# understanding the consequences — once shipped, the bytes are
# part of the contract with every user's DB.
*.sql text eol=lf

# Release notes: bundled into the binary via include_str! and
# rendered in Settings → Updates. Line-ending drift wouldn't crash
# anything (no hash check), but the rendering would noticeably
# change between platforms. Lock for stable rendering. Glob from
# any depth (covers `Utterbar Windows App/releases/<v>.md` without
# fighting gitattributes' escaping rules for spaces in paths).
**/releases/*.md text eol=lf
